# keur security control — a SonarQube token/login committed in
# sonar-project.properties.
#
# Why this exists: `sonar.login` / `sonar.token` is an authentication secret. It
# belongs in a CI secret (passed as -Dsonar.token=$SONAR_TOKEN), never checked
# into the repo. A committed token grants anyone with repo read access the
# ability to push analyses / read project data, and it lingers in git history
# even after removal (CWE-798 hardcoded credentials).
#
# HOW (pattern engine): match `sonar.login` / `sonar.token` assigned a non-empty
# value on the line. The exclude suppresses the safe form where the value is a
# shell/CI variable reference ($SONAR_TOKEN or ${env.SONAR_TOKEN}) rather than a
# literal secret.
#
# WARN (not block) with high confidence: on a true literal this is a real leak,
# but teams occasionally template the file; the exclude covers the common safe
# case so the remaining hits are worth a human look. @see docs/rules-authoring.md
id: SONAR-003
ilities: [confidentiality]
maturity: 2
title: SonarQube token/login committed in sonar-project.properties
category: security
severity: warning
engine: pattern
action: warn
confidence: 0.8
target:
  filenames: sonar-project.properties
  content_contains: sonar.
patterns:
  - regex: (?m)^\s*sonar\.(login|token|password)\s*=\s*\S
    exclude: \$\{?[A-Za-z_]
    message: "SonarQube token/login committed in sonar-project.properties — this is a secret (CWE-798). Remove it and pass it from a CI secret: `-Dsonar.token=$SONAR_TOKEN`. Rotate the token; it stays in git history."
fix: "Delete the sonar.login/sonar.token/sonar.password line from the file and supply the token from a CI secret at scan time (`-Dsonar.token=$SONAR_TOKEN`). Rotate the exposed token."
